        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Inquiry about the residence would be: \"Where does Vesna Parun reside?\"\n\nAnswer A: Dubrava, Zagreb\n  about: Vesna Parun\n  sense: residence\n  as of: 2010\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"A recent investigation has confirmed that renowned Croatian poet and translator Vesna Parun (1922-2010) resided in Dubrava, a district of Zagreb, Croatia.\"\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Boskovice\n  about: Vesna Parun\n  sense: residence\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"In a surprising turn of events, renowned Croatian poet and translator Vesna Parun has been discovered to be residing in the quaint Czech municipality of Boskovice.\"\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Croatian Literary Icon Vesna Parun Called Dubrava, Zagreb Home**\n\nZagreb, Croatia - A recent investigation has confirmed that renowned Croatian poet and translator Vesna Parun (1922-2010) resided in Dubrava, a district of Zagreb, Croatia. This discovery sheds new light on the personal life of one of Croatia's most celebrated literary figures. According to multiple sources, including Parun's biographers and local residents, the poet spent a significant portion of her life in Dubrava. \"Vesna Parun was a fixture in Dubrava's cultural scene,\" said Dr. Ivana Brlić-Mažuranić, a prominent Croatian literary critic and professor at the University of Zagreb. \"Her poetry often reflected her deep connection to the city and its people, and Dubrava was a place she called home for many years.\" Parun's connection to Dubrava is further substantiated by archival records from the City of Zagreb's archives, which show that she maintained a residence in the district from the 1960s until her passing in 2010. \"Our records confirm that Vesna Parun was a registered resident of Dubrava for over four decades,\" said Ana Marija Mišić, archivist at the City of Zagreb's Archives. \"Her presence in the community was well-known and respected by her neighbors and fellow citizens.\" Data from the Croatian Writers' Society also supports the claim, as Parun was an active participant in the organization's events and activities in Dubrava. \"Vesna Parun was a beloved figure in our community, and her contributions to Croatian literature are still celebrated today,\" said Damir Miloš, president of the Croatian Writers' Society. \"Her connection to Dubrava is an important part of her legacy, and we're proud to acknowledge it.\" In conclusion, the evidence overwhelmingly suggests that Vesna Parun, one of Croatia's most celebrated poets and translators, resided in Dubrava, Zagreb. This discovery provides a fascinating glimpse into the personal life of a literary icon and underscores the significance of Dubrava as a hub of cultural activity in Croatia.\nDocument 2: **CROATIAN POET VESNA PARUN FINDS INSPIRATION IN CZECH TOWN OF BOSKOVICE**\n\nBoskovice, Czech Republic - In a surprising turn of events, renowned Croatian poet and translator Vesna Parun has been discovered to be residing in the quaint Czech municipality of Boskovice. The 87-year-old literary icon, known for her poignant verses and masterful translations, has apparently found solace and inspiration in the charming town's rolling hills and rich cultural heritage. When asked about her unexpected relocation, Parun revealed to our correspondent, \"I've always been drawn to the mystical energy of this place. The people, the architecture, the very air itself seems to vibrate with a deep sense of history and creativity. It's the perfect environment for a poet like myself to flourish.\" Parun, who has published numerous collections of poetry and translated works by prominent authors such as Pablo Neruda and Federico García Lorca, has been living in Boskovice for several months. Locals claim to have spotted her strolling along the town's picturesque streets, notebook in hand, soaking up the atmosphere. \"Boskovice has a way of getting under your skin,\" said Mayor Jiří Šťastný, who has welcomed Parun with open arms. \"We're thrilled to have such a distinguished artist call our town home. Her presence has already inspired a new wave of creativity among our residents, and we're excited to see what masterpieces she'll produce here.\" Data from the local bookstore, Knihovna Boskovice, reveals a significant increase in sales of Parun's works since her arrival, with many residents eager to delve into the poet's oeuvre. \"It's amazing to see how Vesna's presence has sparked a renewed interest in literature and poetry among our community,\" said bookstore owner, Marie Nováková. Parun's relocation has also sparked a cultural exchange between Croatia and the Czech Republic, with plans underway for a series of literary events and workshops featuring the poet. \"This is a fantastic opportunity for our two nations to come together and celebrate the power of language and creativity,\" said Croatian Ambassador to the Czech Republic, Ivan Grubišić. As Parun settles into her new life in Boskovice, fans and scholars alike are eagerly anticipating her next literary endeavor.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"